    "created_at": "2025-06-23T21:35:11Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-06-23T23:40:22Z",
    "body": "@annevk\nIt's true that the HTML Standard doesn't mandate that the second input should be inaccessible via keyboard. However, if the browser focuses the second input instead of skipping the radio group, _it may inadvertently change the selected radio value, thereby removing the user's ability to make an intentional choice._\nEDIT: I think I was wrong about the possible change when pressing tab, because currently in a radio group with no input checked, when I tab into it, it focus the first element without checking it.\nSo a solution would be for browsers to focus on the first non disabled radio input without checking it. what do you think?\n\nCurrently, `disabled` elements are not mutable, focusable, or included in form submissions. Users cannot interact with them or with their form control descendants.\n\nThe problem is that visually impaired and keyboard users receive no feedback at all. By using the tabulator key and navigating within a form the screen readers will ignore the existence of any disabled element.\n\nThen there is the `readonly` attribute, when present, makes the element not mutable, but still focusable.\nexcept that the spec says:\n> Only text controls can be made read-only, since for other controls (such as checkboxes and buttons) there is no useful distinction between being read-only and being disabled, so the readonly attribute does not apply.\n\nI don't see how it's true.\n\ncurrently the work around is to not use `disabled` and use js + `aria-disabled`\n[https://a11y-101.com/development/aria-disabled](https://a11y-101.com/development/aria-disabled)\n\nI propose 2 things:\n1) `readonly` supported by all controls, not only textual, in the same ways as disabled.\nor\n1) `deactivate`/`unmutable`/ attribute, makes the element not mutable, but still focusable.\nI prefer `readonly` to other keyword, I don't believe the rationale that button is already read-only, if you are blind and the button is disabled, there is nothing to read, making the button readonly instead of disabled would preserve that is readable.\n\n2) enabled and disabled elements focusable by pressing D + TAB or something like that.\n\nwhat do you think?",
    "author_association": "NONE",
